"Biographies of Zhang Shizhi and Feng Tang"

Zhang Shizhi, the Commandant of Justice, was a native of Duyang, with the courtesy name Ji. He lived together with his elder brother Zhong. He became a cavalry gentleman through a property qualification, serving Emperor Xiaowen. For ten years he received no promotion and remained unknown. Shizhi said, "Having been an official for a long time has depleted Zhong's property, and I have not achieved anything." He wanted to resign and return home. Zhonglang General Yuan Ang knew his worth and regretted his departure, so he requested that Shizhi be transferred to fill the position of Usher. After the morning audience, Shizhi took the opportunity to speak on matters of convenience. Emperor Wen said, "Keep it down-to-earth; do not make lofty speeches, but make it something that can be implemented now." So Shizhi spoke about the affairs between the Qin and Han dynasties, why Qin lost and why Han prospered, for a long time. Emperor Wen praised him and appointed Shizhi as Supervisor of Ushers.

Shizhi accompanied the emperor on a trip to the Tiger Enclosure. The emperor asked the Shanglin Commandant about the registers of various birds and animals. After more than ten questions, the commandant looked left and right, unable to answer at all. The Tiger Enclosure Keeper, standing by, answered the emperor's questions about the registers in great detail on behalf of the commandant, wanting to show his ability to respond fluently without end. Emperor Wen said, "Shouldn't an official be like this? The commandant is worthless!" He then ordered Shizhi to appoint the keeper as the Shanglin Director. Shizhi, after a long pause, stepped forward and said, "Your Majesty, what kind of man is the Marquis of Jiang, Zhou Bo?" The emperor said, "An elder of integrity." He then asked, "What kind of man is the Marquis of Dongyang, Zhang Xiangru?" The emperor again said, "An elder of integrity." Shizhi said, "Now the Marquis of Jiang and the Marquis of Dongyang are called elders, yet these two men could hardly speak when discussing affairs. How could they imitate this keeper with his glib tongue and sharp speech! Moreover, because Qin employed petty scribes, they vied with each other in being hasty and harsh, but their fault was that it was merely a formality, without any genuine compassion. Therefore, they did not hear of their faults, and the decline continued until the Second Emperor, when the empire collapsed. Now, if Your Majesty promotes this keeper because of his glib tongue, I fear that the whole empire will follow suit, vying for glibness without substance. Moreover, the influence of the superior on the inferior is faster than shadow and echo; your actions and decisions must be carefully considered." Emperor Wen said, "Good." So he stopped and did not appoint the keeper.

When the emperor got into his carriage, he summoned Shizhi to ride with him. Driving slowly, he asked Shizhi about the faults of Qin. Shizhi answered truthfully and in detail. When they reached the palace, the emperor appointed Shizhi as Chief of the Palace Gate.

Soon after, the Crown Prince and the King of Liang rode together in a carriage to the court, and did not dismount at the Sima Gate. Shizhi then pursued and stopped them, preventing them from entering the palace gate. He then impeached them for disrespect in not dismounting at the palace gate and submitted the memorial. When Empress Dowager Bo heard of this, Emperor Wen removed his cap and apologized, saying, "I have not taught my sons properly." Empress Dowager Bo then sent an envoy with an edict to pardon the Crown Prince and the King of Liang, and only then were they allowed to enter. From this, Emperor Wen considered Shizhi extraordinary and appointed him as Palace Counselor.

Soon after, he became Zhonglang General. He accompanied the emperor to Baling, and they stood on the northern edge overlooking the latrine. At that time, Consort Shen was accompanying the emperor. The emperor pointed out the road to Xinfeng to Consort Shen and said, "This is the road to Handan." He had Consort Shen play the zither, and the emperor himself leaned on the zither and sang, his mood sad and mournful. He turned to his ministers and said, "Alas! If I use the stone of the northern mountain for my outer coffin, and fill the gaps with chopped ramie and silk, and seal it with lacquer, how could it be moved!" Those around all said, "Excellent." Shizhi stepped forward and said, "If there is something desirable inside, even if you seal it up in the southern mountain, there will still be a crack; if there is nothing desirable inside, even without a stone outer coffin, what is there to worry about!" Emperor Wen praised him. Afterwards, he appointed Shizhi as Commandant of Justice.

Soon after, the emperor was traveling and came out of the Zhongwei Bridge. A man ran out from under the bridge, startling the emperor's carriage horses. The emperor then sent cavalry to arrest him and handed him over to the Commandant of Justice. Shizhi interrogated him. The man said, "I am a man from the county. Hearing the imperial procession, I hid under the bridge. After a long time, thinking the procession had passed, I came out. Seeing the carriage and cavalry, I just ran." The Commandant of Justice submitted his judgment: one man violating the imperial procession should be fined. Emperor Wen was angry and said, "This man personally startled my horses. Fortunately, my horses are gentle. If it had been other horses, wouldn't I have been injured? Yet the Commandant of Justice only fines him!" Shizhi said, "The law is something the Son of Heaven shares with all under heaven. Now the law is like this, but if you change it to a heavier penalty, the law will not be trusted by the people. Moreover, at that time, if Your Majesty had ordered his immediate execution, that would have been the end of it. But now that you have handed him over to the Commandant of Justice, the Commandant of Justice is the balance of all under heaven. If it tilts, then all those who apply the law will use it lightly or heavily, and where will the people place their hands and feet? I beg Your Majesty to consider this." After a long time, the emperor said, "The Commandant of Justice's judgment is correct."

Later, someone stole the jade ring from the seat of the Gaozu Temple. The thief was caught, and Emperor Wen was angry, handing him over to the Commandant of Justice for trial. Shizhi, according to the law on stealing items used in the imperial ancestral temple, submitted a memorial, and the judgment was execution in the marketplace. The emperor was greatly angered and said, "This man is so lawless as to steal the vessel of the First Emperor's temple. I handed him over to the Commandant of Justice because I wanted to have him executed along with his family, but you have submitted a judgment according to the law. This is not my intention in reverently serving the ancestral temple." Shizhi removed his cap, kowtowed, and apologized, saying, "The law is sufficient as it is. Moreover, even if the crime is the same, the punishment is graded according to the degree of offense. Now, if you execute the family of a man who stole a temple vessel, suppose, by a ten-thousandth chance, a foolish man takes a handful of earth from Changling, what law would Your Majesty add to punish him?" After a long time, the emperor discussed it with the Empress Dowager and then approved the Commandant of Justice's judgment. At that time, the Commandant of Justice, the Marquis of Tiao, Zhou Yafu, and the Chancellor of Liang, the Marquis of Shandu, Wang Tiankai, saw that Shizhi held to fair judgments, and they became close friends. From this, Zhang Shizhi was praised throughout the empire.

Later, Emperor Wen died, and Emperor Jing ascended the throne. Shizhi was afraid and feigned illness. He wanted to resign, but feared severe punishment; he wanted to go and apologize, but did not know what the outcome would be. Following Wang Sheng's plan, he finally went to apologize, and Emperor Jing did not punish him.

Wang Sheng was skilled in the teachings of the Yellow Emperor and Laozi, and was a retired scholar. He was once summoned to the court, where the Three Dukes and Nine Ministers were all assembled. Wang Sheng, an old man, said, "My sock is loose," and turning to Zhang Shizhi, said, "Tie my sock for me!" Shizhi knelt and tied it. Afterwards, someone said to Wang Sheng, "Why did you insult Zhang Shizhi in court, making him kneel to tie your sock?" Wang Sheng said, "I am old and lowly, and I know I can be of no use to Zhang Shizhi. Zhang Shizhi is now a famous minister of the empire. I deliberately humiliated him a little, making him kneel to tie my sock, in order to enhance his reputation." When the lords heard this, they considered Wang Sheng virtuous and respected Zhang Shizhi even more.

Zhang Shizhi served Emperor Jing for a little over a year, then became the Chancellor of the King of Huainan, still because of his previous offense. After a long time, Shizhi died. His son was Zhang Zhi, courtesy name Zhanggong, who reached the rank of Grandee but was dismissed. Because he could not ingratiate himself with the contemporary world, he remained unemployed for the rest of his life.

Feng Tang was a native of Zhao by ancestry. His father moved to Dai. After the Han arose, they moved to Anling. Tang was known for his filial piety and served as Chief of the Langzhong Bureau, serving Emperor Wen. Emperor Wen passed by in his carriage and asked Tang, "How did you, an elder, become a Lang? Where is your home?" Tang answered truthfully. Emperor Wen said, "When I was in Dai, my Supervisor of Food, Gao Que, often spoke to me about the worthiness of the Zhao general Li Qi, and his battles at Julu. Now, whenever I eat, my thoughts are always on Julu. Do you know him, elder?" Tang replied, "He is still not as good as Lian Po and Li Mu as generals." The emperor said, "Why?" Tang said, "When my grandfather was in Zhao, he was a commander of troops and was on good terms with Li Mu. My father was formerly the Chancellor of Dai and was on good terms with the Zhao general Li Qi, and knew his character." When the emperor heard about the character of Lian Po and Li Mu, he was very pleased and slapped his thigh, saying, "Alas! If only I could have Lian Po and Li Mu as my generals, why would I worry about the Xiongnu!" Tang said, "I beg your pardon! Even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them." The emperor was angry, rose, and went into the palace. After a long time, he summoned Tang and rebuked him, saying, "Why did you humiliate me in public? Could you not find a private place?" Tang apologized, saying, "I am a rustic and do not know taboos."

At that time, the Xiongnu had just made a major incursion into Chaona, killing the Commandant of Beidi, Ang. The emperor was concerned about the barbarian invasion, so he finally asked Tang again, "How do you know that I cannot use Lian Po and Li Mu?" Tang replied, "I have heard that in ancient times, when a king sent out a general, he would kneel and push the chariot wheel, saying, 'Within the gate, I control; outside the gate, the general controls.' Military merits, ranks, and rewards were all decided outside and reported upon return. This is not an empty saying. My grandfather said that when Li Mu was a general on the Zhao border, the taxes from the military markets were all used by himself to feast his soldiers, and rewards were decided outside, without interference from the court. He was entrusted with full responsibility and held accountable for success. Therefore, Li Mu was able to fully use his intelligence and ability. He deployed 1,300 war chariots, 13,000 mounted archers, and 100,000 elite soldiers worth a hundred gold pieces. Thus, he drove the Chanyu north, defeated the Donghu, annihilated the Danlin, suppressed the strong Qin in the west, and held off Han and Wei in the south. At that time, Zhao almost became hegemon. Later, King Zhao Qian ascended the throne. His mother was a singer. King Qian, upon ascending, listened to the slander of Guo Kai and eventually executed Li Mu, appointing Yan Ju in his place. Therefore, the army was defeated, the soldiers fled, and Zhao was captured and destroyed by Qin. Now I have heard privately that Wei Shang, the Governor of Yunzhong, used all the taxes from the military markets to feast his soldiers, and spent his own private funds, slaughtering an ox every five days to feast his guests, military officers, and retainers. Therefore, the Xiongnu kept far away and did not approach the Yunzhong border. Once, the barbarians did invade, and Shang led his chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many. The soldiers were all commoners' sons, who had left their fields to join the army. How could they know the military registers and unit rosters? They fought hard all day, cutting off heads and capturing prisoners, and when reporting merits to the headquarters, if one word did not match, the civil officials would punish them according to the law. Their rewards were not given, but the officials' laws were always applied. I am foolish and believe that Your Majesty's laws are too clear, rewards too light, and punishments too heavy. Moreover, Wei Shang, the Governor of Yunzhong, was convicted for reporting the number of enemy heads taken as six fewer than the actual count. Your Majesty handed him over to the officials, stripped him of his rank, and sentenced him to hard labor. From this, I say that even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them. I am truly foolish and have touched upon taboos. I deserve death, I deserve death!" Emperor Wen was pleased. That very day, he ordered Feng Tang to take the imperial credentials and pardon Wei Shang, reinstating him as Governor of Yunzhong, and appointed Tang as Chief of Chariot and Cavalry, in charge of the chariot soldiers of the Commandant of the Capital and the commanderies and kingdoms.

Seven years later, Emperor Jing ascended the throne. He appointed Tang as Chancellor of Chu, but he was dismissed. When Emperor Wu ascended the throne, he sought worthy and excellent men, and Feng Tang was recommended. Tang was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve as an official. So Tang's son Feng Sui was made a Gentleman. Sui, courtesy name Wangsun, was also an extraordinary man and was on good terms with me.

The Grand Historian says: Zhang Ji's words about elders, upholding the law without currying favor; Feng Gong's discussion of generals and commanders—how meaningful! How meaningful! The saying goes, "If you do not know a man, look at his friends." The praises recited by these two gentlemen are worthy of being placed in the court. The Book of Documents says, "Without partiality, without partisanship, the kingly way is broad; without partisanship, without partiality, the kingly way is smooth." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong came close to this. [end of text]


